  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions continue along with much warmer \nair arriving through the start of the weekend.\n\nAs an upper-level ridge across the Plains builds eastward \nthrough the remainder of this week, it will continue to direct \nsprawling surface high pressure over our region. This surface \nhigh however will eventually become more centered to our south \nand east late this week and especially into the start of the \nweekend. \n\nDry conditions are expected to continue right into the start of\nthe weekend. This will be accompanied by warming temperatures \nwith the warmth looking to peak on Friday as some areas top out \nnear 70 degrees (some 10-15 degrees above average for this time \nof year). It will still be chilly at night, with lows in the \nupper 20s to 30s. A rather dry air mass looks to remain in place\ndue to the upper-level ridge and surface high, however the \nsurface dew points should start to increase especially on \nSaturday as some moisture begins to increase as a light onshore \nflow becomes more established.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring a potential coastal low for the \nsecond half of the weekend into early next week. \n\nAfter surface high pressure shifts offshore on Saturday, most \nguidance depict a coastal low developing off the coast of the \nSoutheast U.S. over the weekend. While there are great \ndifferences amongst the timing, track and intensity of this \npotential system, there is a general consensus in a low \ndeveloping. In fact, AI guidance has been fairly consistent with\nthe low tracking over/within close vicinity of the Mid-Atlantic\nwhile other ensemble guidance keeps the system well offshore. \nWhile it is way too early for specifics of what 'potential' \nimpacts may be felt around the region, it is worth watching as \nthis may be the next chance to bring some beneficial rainfall to\nthe region. PoPs are currently around 30-50%.",
